### auth_lab.http — Authentication Gauntlet Lab
### Run a block with Ctrl+Alt+R (Windows/Linux) or Cmd+Alt+R (macOS).
### All endpoints live under http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 1 — Basic Authentication
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (unauthenticated — should return 401)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1

###

### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (with credentials — admin:admin123 in Base64)
# REST Client sends the raw Authorization header exactly as written below.
# YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM= is Base64("admin:admin123")
# Check your terminal after sending — the server prints the raw header.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=

###

### Phase 1 — Synthesis: Replay Attack (no password needed)
# An attacker captured this exact header off the network (plain HTTP = no TLS).
# They do NOT need to decode it — they simply copy and replay the same header.
# This works because Base64 is reversible ENCODING, not ENCRYPTION.
# The server cannot tell this apart from the real client's request.
# Lesson: Base64 provides ZERO security — TLS (HTTPS) is the only real shield.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 3 — Token Authentication (Opaque)
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### Phase 3 — Opaque Token Auth (valid token — should return 200)
# Token was generated with: python manage.py drf_create_token admin
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/token/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Token e5a49e294f433ee80400911c01307a8493fec1f4


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 4 — JSON Web Tokens (JWT)
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Phase 4 — JWT Login (obtain access + refresh tokens)
# Send username and password → server returns access and refresh tokens.
# After running, copy the "access" value and paste it into @accessToken below.
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/login/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "username": "admin",
    "password": "admin123"
}

###

@accessToken = <PASTE_ACCESS_TOKEN_HERE>

### Phase 4 — JWT Protected Request (valid token — should return 200)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/protected/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###

### Phase 4 — Tampered JWT (user_id changed to 2 via jwt.io — should return 401)
# 1. Go to https://jwt.io and paste the access token into the Encoded box.
# 2. In the Payload section, change "user_id" from 1 to 2.
# 3. Copy the new Encoded token and paste it below.
# The server rejects it because the signature was computed over the ORIGINAL
# payload — changing user_id breaks the cryptographic signature.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/protected/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer <PASTE_TAMPERED_TOKEN_HERE>

###

### Phase 4 — JWT Refresh (bonus: get a new access token using the refresh token)
@refreshToken = <PASTE_REFRESH_TOKEN_HERE>

POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/jwt/refresh/ HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "refresh": "{{refreshToken}}"
}
